5. Temporal Selves

Death opens up the question of Being. 
(Martin Heidegger, Being and Time)

Somehow, we feel, the water of the physical brain is turned into the wine of consciousness, but we draw a total blank on the nature of this conversion.
(Colin McGinn, "Can We Solve the Mind-Body Problem?")

The third auxiliary is consciousness. Consciousness is taken to be a mystery, perhaps even one of the most important mysteries. McGinn compares the development of consciousness to transmuting water into wine. Something truly magical. But we should be clear on what’s being discussed. We seem to have in mind what I would call the magnificent view of consciousness: that is, thinking about thinking, exploring abstract concepts, contemplating the universe, deciding, creating, catching glimpses of the inner machinery inside our heads. These are some impressive feats. We don’t think this to simply involve having memories and emotions. There is no big mystery to how physical memories, for instance, cause behavior. Well, it is a big mystery, but it is not the real bread and butter of the mind-body problem.  The magnificent view of consciousness, instead, I take it, has to do with ideas. Ideas are the magic, the wine. But this wine is not transmuted but rather mixed and diluted in the water of memories. Only then is it potable. In the next section I try to make sense of ideas, but to be clear I am in no way trying to solve the mind-body problem.  So in getting some work done here, let’s put the mind-body problem and the magnificent view of consciousness aside. Instead, I want to try to tell a story of how consciousness might have come about. The details and cogitation may be wrong, but all of the things I will discuss did happen, at some point, in some capacity.
	Finding the right starting point or threshold for consciousness is difficult. Mere awareness is not substantive enough to matter; when it comes to what humans do, and talk about minds. Many animals are clearly conscious in the general sense, being aware of both their environment and their own bodies. Thus, we must find some point to draw a sharp distinction, without simply adopting the magnificent view of consciousness. I take a substantive criterion of consciousness to be this: being aware that you are aware of something. This has some potential. Though I will expand on it. But I take this to be something we do, and animals don't. The first task is to build up to this.  
	Let us throw our unconscious ancestors into the world, armed with only a reasonably big brain, complex emotions, and memories (to include access to a collective unconscious and a number of archetypes). What would immersion in this world be like? I take it to involve more than empty physicality. Emotion and archetypes would give natural objects value. The Sun is not a light in the sky, but giver of life. The trees sing. The birds watch over us. I am tempted to say the natural objects are ready-to-hand, in Heidegger's usage of the term.  But that doesn't quite capture it. Our ancestors were not immersed in tools to be used. But the natural objects, like tools, are given purposes. It is hard to point to exactly what these purposes were, except to say they were focuses of reverence. And this reverence, I take it, played an important role in our ancestors' lives, shaping how they interacted with the world and each other. The things ready-to-hand, then, for our ancestors, were not tools, but rather physical icons, in which the mental components of these icons are the archetypes in our heads. But it is not quite right to call the icons artifacts, on account of these natural objects not actually being created by humans. Though the influence is similar. 
	Besides awe (and fear), a consequence of living in this world would be the feeling of smallness. Things are much bigger than you can comprehend. This, I believe, caused a feeling/emotion of helplessness and anxiety in our ancestors. The world was too important to lose. Nor did they want to leave it. Facing the world, in this way, I hold, with a few concessions, leads an individual to confront their own mortality. But this is not simply awareness of death itself. Clearly our ancestors were aware that their kin were dying, probably quite often. Rather, in the Heideggerian sense, it would involve facing one's own death. Or as Heidegger would refer to it, being-towards-death. 
	Being-towards-death involves seeing death as one's possibility. Death is the “possibility of the impossibility of any existence at all,” as Heidegger says.  What this means is that death is forward-looking. But at the same time immanent. Death looms over us, and is always possible, at any time. It is the end of existence but also the focus of existence. In this way, for Heidegger, we anticipate death.  But in doing so we see existence as finite, and having a definite end. This shapes the forward-looking aspect of death to be inescapable and belonging to us. In this way, my death is mine. Here we are aware of death, as always possible; something that can happen to us, in the future at any moment, even though death is not here now. In this way, we define ourself by the future and finitude. In analyzing our own anticipation (and dread) we become aware that we are aware of something, mainly, that we will/must die and that existence will/must come to an end. From this, I take it, we can form a conception or idea of Time, and supplementary add our place in it. Time then attaches itself to our phenomenal experience, eventually becoming embedded into the frame of consciousness itself. We become aware that we are now aware of something—again, our own deaths. Thus, the proposed criterion of consciousness can be satisfied. 
	But the above type of consciousness is not necessarily the same as self-consciousness or personal identity. For example, you can be aware that you're experiencing something or aware that you are something or aware that you will not be something without reference to individuality. To get individuality will take some more steps. One small leap, would be to allow that increases in intelligence would ignite improvements in memory to the point where our ancestors could be aware that they were aware of something in a similar way to how they're currently aware. With the idea of Time this would not be an unreasonable development. With this, we could have some conception, however small, of a continuity of self. Although, deriving a personal self or an idea of Self requires more work. One move would be to hold that consciousness will value itself, or hold itself as valuable, even while remaining a mystery to itself. But this is difficult to argue for. Though most organisms will value their own life, or continued existence, in principle. 
	The next task is volition. Without being aware that you are the source of your actions your consciousness is like a spectator watching its own concerto. In searching for an origin of volition or a rudimentary form of volition we might look to Arthur Schopenhauer. Schopenhauer in his openly pessimistic work, The World as Will and Representation, claims the base of existence is an incessant impulse. He calls this will, and is very hostile toward it. He holds this will to be entirely irrational, aimless, blind, uncaused.  But if what he says is right, and the will is blind and beyond causality and even time , as he claims, then it should follow that all will is free. Unless we want to simply stipulate that a free will must directly come from or result from consciousness.  I will later argue that consciousness does not necessarily make us more free; in fact the reverse.  But this need not be a topic of contention at this stage of the argument. 
	What we can do, is propose a conception of agency, where a being who is aware that they are the cause of their own actions is taken to be responsible for those actions. Here we have a type of conscious agency. But, I should point out, rather confusedly, that actual acts of the will are not required for this to work, or at least we need not commit, at this stage, to there being a type of will that acts freely. Instead, being aware that you are the cause of your own actions, in itself, makes you responsible for anything that comes from you, intentional or unintentional; even admitting that many acts, by you, are not done freely. With this in mind, we can form a conception of Self as a whole being, in the world, rather than reduce it to a mysterious point of will or volition. In recognizing this, the idea of Self becomes possible and comprehensible. But this idea of Self is not yet a personal self, or a distinctive finite self. 
	For whatever reason, it seems, it is easier to imagine an infinite self over a finite self. An infinite self, I take it, would be "God," to use the familiar term for such a thing. In this development, God becomes the cause and explanation of all the mysteries in the world; including you. In this way, anxiety is relieved and responsibility is diminished. The angst involved in being an agent is limited and reduced to what you happen to decide you would like to have control over. We create the personal self as a flawed and imperfect being—always justified in evading responsibility, despite being a center of volition and the pinnacle of his/her universe. By relinquishing control in this way, we give God immense power. And this is the state we currently find ourselves in. Hence, we have the lens to view, and the black powder to create the idea of Self and the idea of God. Though both will be shapeshifters in countless forms. 
	We have arrived, in outline, at some of the most important primitive ideas for human beings: Time, Self, God. I propose some other primitive ideas would be: Being (permanence), Change (impermanence), Cause/Effect (volition generalized), Nothingness (death generalized), Necessity, Potentiality, Other (including the recognition of other selves), Quantity (with abstraction), Likeness/Difference, Proximity (both continuous and non-continuous), Quality (distinctness), Order (real or imagined), and Relation.
	With these primitive ideas the Conscious can take shape. And we will eventually find ourselves unable to think at all without them. Thus, we have far exceeded what was necessary to demarcate ourselves from animals. But we should demand no less. This is, in fact, the human being we know! Though a dose of rationalism is required for my schema to work. And, to the frustration of metaphysicians, these primitive ideas, unfortunately, escape any proper definitions—in language anyway. 


6. Genealogy of Ideas

All is number. 
(Pythagoras, — )

It is not altogether unreasonable to describe digital computers as brains.
(Alan Turing, "Can Digital Computers Think?")

Ideas, as was stated, require consciousness. But we should acknowledge that Consciousness forces the world to present itself a certain way. At its most fundamental level consciousness produces the Laws of Logic. But it's important to point out that the laws of logic do not necessarily apply to the world but rather to the Mind. The most important laws of logic are the Law of Identity, the Law of Excluded Middle, and the Law of Noncontradiction.  As far as ideas are concerned, in particular their capacity to be transmitted to others, the most consequential law of logic is the Law of Noncontradiction. 
	The Law of Noncontradiction holds that a thing is not what it is not, or more succinctly: A ≠ not A. We should add to it: at the same time. This is extremely important, because without the addition of temporality the law of noncontradiction lacks teeth.  But this proves extraneous to what should be allowed to enter into supposedly self-evident axioms. To make it work, I contend, we ought to/must bring in the primitive idea of Time, which was argued for in the previous section. If what was said is correct, even if only in some capacity, we have the means to extend the laws of logic; the law of noncontradiction at least; outside of one’s own mind. Thus, the idea of Time proves paramount to the development of all non-primitive ideas.
	Non-primitive ideas, I take it, are ideas that can, in principle, be communicated. On account of this fact (with non-contradiction and time) these types of ideas must be binary. That is, in formulating ideas, a thing either is what you take it to be or not what you take it to be.  Within this binary framework, I propose that ideas are akin to programs; and words are simply code that we exchange with one another. The computer analogy of the mind is well-trodden. Though I hope to do something a little different with it. And even if the analogy is overused, I take it to be the right one and the best approximation that we have to how ideas work. Now, ideas are complex. They are not simply mental images or collections of factual claims, as many people take them to be. Ideas, being programs, must run and interpret vast amounts of information. Ideas are not the information itself, but rather the manifestation of that information to consciousness in a clear and ordered model. Only certain configurations are possible for each thing, meaning we will come to a convergence on which ideas can be properly run. That is, unlike memories, which are unique to each individual, ideas, for the most part, are the same for everyone. We all have the same manifestations of each Idea. And this is true independent of language or how they are conveyed. Just as different types of code can run the same program, different words can produce the same Idea. Largely, the same Idea can be multiply realized.
	This may sound a little strange at first. For example, let's say you've spent quite some time staring at an oak tree. You go to tell your friend about the exercise. You say you've gathered some insights by staring at a 'tree'. You, of course, might have some mental image of the particular tree that you are referring to. But that is lost in the process of communicating it. When hearing 'tree' your friend might also think of a particular mental image of some different tree. But the point is neither of those mental images matter. They are mere memories and not what is being communicated. And this would be the case even if thousands of mental images were brought to mind. The idea is something above. What is shared is a type of configuration that is either met or not met. If you described the tree as having tentacles, for instance, then this configuration would not be met, and your friend would likely ask for some clarification on what you mean by 'tree'. The actual idea is somewhat obscure, even if we always have some mental image that is drawn to make sense of it through representation of sense impressions. The program simply reads the code, even if you don't understand it. Its true nature is non-sensory.
	A consequence of this is that a configuration, or Ideas, potentially exist in principle without the mind. Though minds are needed to actualize them. For example, the Idea of Tree would remain as a configuration that can be met, even if all speakers disappeared. If speakers eventually returned, say after a long time, the same Idea can be communicated even if the language or code was drastically different from before. Again, Ideas can be multiply realized by different code. If this is possible, then it must be the case that ideas/programs are non-physical. 
	In his influential essay, "Minds, Brains, and Programs," John Searle starkly warns that we should be reluctant to accept the possibility that a program can be separated from its hardware, and function as a mind, because it amounts to endorsing dualism. I have no problem calling Searle’s bluff. Yes, dualism is true and programs/ideas are non-physical in the genuine sense. Though some further clarification is in order about what I mean by ideas being non-physical.
	By non-physical I mean that ideas don’t exist fully or only partly exist. I take ideas to be pure potentialities. What this means is that: when not being consciously reinforced, ideas are not part of the physical world. Though how consciousness allows ideas to be actualized, and become real, is hard to say. But it need not be too hard to imagine. For instance, a favorite example of Aristotle, is that an acorn is potentially an oak tree, and has this potential before any oak tree actually exists.  The oak tree in some sense does exist, just at a different time. By ideas being non-physical I also mean they are mind-dependent. Ideas cannot be actualized, or at least known, outside the presence of consciousness and memories. Though, unlike memories, ideas do not take up space, or exist in space. But Ideas nonetheless are still anchored in/to memories and the mind. By anchored I mean temporally anchored. Ideas do continue to exist in time, even if not in space. They remain entangled with the right future memory(ies). If pressed, I would admit that’s what I mean by partial existence rather than non-existence or full existence. And this could function as a working definition of what non-physical things are. 
	All that being said, even though ideas are non-physical, in the genuine sense, I hold, they still possess causal powers. The primary instrument of ideas, for interacting with the world, and the human organism, is the rational faculty or Reason. Reason is a source of volition involving ideas. It utilizes ideas to direct memories and influence behavior. Some of the strongest influencers there are come from Ideas. That said, we should acknowledge most of our actions are based on memories, or experience, rather than reason or ideas. There is nothing inherently wrong with this. Commonsense beliefs and habit are useful guides. They just lack the force of a rational sanction. But I’ll talk about Reason and action (and importantly, also emotion) in more detail later. 
	Ideas can also be contemplated, whatever that entails, through a source of conscious inner volition. This is somewhat of a mystery and different from examining a projected mental image or recalling a memory. I speculate it involves finding necessary connections and overlap between ideas. There are programs within larger programs, so to speak. The further and more abstract we go, the closer we get to understanding the workings of the mind. The goal, in theory, would be to find the source code(s) of all Ideas you house. This eventually, I admit, becomes an esoteric exercise; but if I had to guess, the source code(s) probably involves the primitive ideas; with the master Idea being Time or God—even though God contributes nothing new outside of Himself.
	In closing, I acknowledge a likely response to the kind of reasoning I am employing would be to compare my treatment of ideas to Platonic Forms.  The comparison is almost irresistible, and has been made time and time again throughout history to similar types of arguments. But such a comparison would be misguided. While I agree that Forms/ideas are immaterial and even that there may be some special immaterial Reservoir that could house ideas in something like the active intellect , I don’t take it that Forms/ideas are the ultimate cause of the physical world or that there is some separate better ideal reality. In fact, I take it, Forms/ideas have less reality than physical objects, or even less reality than memories. Let’s not succumb to Plato’s 2,500-year-old trap, as beautiful as it may be.
